        "explanation": "Explanation:\nTest case 111: Here A=[1,2,3]A = [1, 2, 3]A=[1,2,3] and P=1P = 1P=1:\n\nscore(A[1,1],1)=1(A[1,1],1) = 1(A[1,1],1)=1 since we choose the index 111 and subtract 111 from A1A_1A1​. Similarly score(A[2,2],1)=2(A[2,2],1) = 2(A[2,2],1)=2 and score(A[3,3],1)=3(A[3,3],1) = 3(A[3,3],1)=3.\nscore(A[1,2],1)=2(A[1,2],1) = 2(A[1,2],1)=2:\n\nFor the first operation, we choose 222 indices i1=1i_1 = 1i1​=1 and i2=2i_2 = 2i2​=2 and subtract 111 from both elements. The array becomes [0,1][0, 1][0,1].\nFor the second operation, we choose 111 index i1=2i_1 = 2i1​=2 and subtract 111 from Ai1A_{i_1}Ai1​​. The array becomes [0,0][0, 0][0,0].\n\n\nscore(A[2,3],1)=3(A[2,3],1) = 3(A[2,3],1)=3\nscore(A[1,2,3],1)=3(A[1,2,3],1) = 3(A[1,2,3],1)=3\n\nThus, sum of scores 1+2+3+2+3+3=141+2+3+2+3+3 = 141+2+3+2+3+3=14.\nTest case 222: For all subarrays, we need 000 operations since all elements are already 000.\nThus, sum of scores is 000."
